                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "5f7dc3c4-3a31-4005-a5d0-609d99aa06a5",
                                "sense_id": "e04061a5-1a1a-39b2-a0ce-dfda87c1b674",
                                "text": "That is the sluff of a rattler; we must be careful."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "d8833c2a-e57b-39b8-a604-749212bda52f",
                        "entry_id": "300da296-2f1d-37ac-95ec-8151d4020cd7",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Alternative spelling of slough (dead skin on a sore or ulcer)."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "alt-of",
                            "alternative"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "f323e110-c8de-4f23-88c7-098056ffc501",
                                "sense_id": "d8833c2a-e57b-39b8-a604-749212bda52f",
                                "text": "This is the sluff that came off of his skin after the burn."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "13aaf558-88b3-3b7a-b256-dd1f7d1ffb1c",
                        "entry_id": "300da296-2f1d-37ac-95ec-8151d4020cd7",
                        "glosses": [
                            "An avalanche, mudslide, or a like slumping of material or debris."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "44af353c-3444-4eaf-8afb-24f0a3276ae3",
                                "sense_id": "13aaf558-88b3-3b7a-b256-dd1f7d1ffb1c",
                                "text": "The Scouts learned from expert Keith Burke that snow avalanches come in different forms. A powder or \"sluff\" avalanche starts at a single point and fans out as surface snow slides downhill. These avalanches are not as violent as slab avalanches. Slab avalanches are very dangerous. They occur when a whole hillside of snow breaks loose in a giant slab, which then breaks into snow blocks that tumble downhill faster and faster."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "973103fe-7c4e-49e7-a3a0-fd0f4b50dd4b",
                                "sense_id": "13aaf558-88b3-3b7a-b256-dd1f7d1ffb1c",
                                "text": "At least for small sluffs like the ones Brown and his colleagues have triggered, the avalanche slides like a block of material instead of flowing like a fluid."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
